  "query_preview": "..\n\nA weak upper-level ridge briefly builds in through Today, \nbringing cool and mostly dry conditions across the region. On \nSunday, the main closed upper-level we have been tracking over the\npast several days continues to correct slower and further \nnorthwest. As a result, QPF continues to drop and temperatures \nhave gradually moderated as the signal grows for strong \nsouthwesterly winds Sunday and Monday area wide. Sunday remains \nthe primary day for precipitation and impacts, with shower \nactivity largely focused along the Hi-line and Rocky Mountain \nFront closest to the low. Further south, an embedded shortwave \nlooking to primarily impact north-central Montana Sunday evening \ninto early Monday morning. Despite limited instability, decent \nlow-level lapse rates and mid-level forcing will support the \npotential for a few weakly convective showers. Model soundings \nshow the classic inverted-v profiled, which combined with 40-50kt \n700mb winds, will allow showers to transfer stronger winds down \nto the surface. While the severe threat is low, localized strong \nwinds gusts remain a possibility with any of the stronger showers \nor \"storms\". \n\nOn Monday, the low exits but low level winds remain elevated.\nMid-week, models are showing a healthy signal for another upper-\nlevel low pressure to try and swing through the forecast area.\nUncertainty in regards to timing and strength is high but worth\nkeeping an eye on as it could bring additional chances of\nprecipitation or at least unsettled weather. Beyond mid-week, our\npattern remains locked in place with ridging anchored over the\nSouth-Central Plains bringing multiple opportunities for weak\nshortwaves to move through the regional flow. Additionally, models\nare hinting at a stronger trough to attempted to push into the\nPacific Northwest before becoming a cut-off low introducing\nadditional uncertainty into the extended forecast. \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nConfidence is high that winter weather impacts will be negligible.\nSnowfall of 1-3\" remains strictly confined to high mountain peaks\nalong the RMF with no impacts to road surface or passes. If the\nlow pressure continues its trend of NW, QPF amounts will continue\nto slip as we lose the forcing needed for more widespread\nprecipitation. \n\nConfidence is moderate, but growing, in regards to elevated\nsurface winds Sunday into Monday. The new orientation and track of\nthe upper-low will increase surface winds across the area,\nbringing widespread gusts over 30kt especially in the Plains and \nSouthwest flow aligned valleys in Southwest Montana. Madison River\nValley looks particularly favorable for winds gusting upwards of\n55kts thus the placement of the High Wind Watch. \n\nConfidence in the general forecast past mid-week remains low.\nQuestions in regards to upper-level pattern remain aplenty with a\nwide array of potential from a cut-off low plaguing the region to\ncontinued rounds of swift moving shortwaves. In the instance the \ncut-off scenario takes place, generally expect prolonged period of\nunsettled weather and cooler temperatures with increased cloud \ncover. On the flip side, if the wave remains open than well see \nmuch of the same with the current pattern. Quick moving \nshortwaves bringing new rounds of weather to watch and \ntemperatures much more seasonal. \n\n-JR",
